Object literals with plain fields: built in one step from a template

An object literal of name: value fields only (no computed names, spread, methods, accessors or __proto__, distinct
names that are not array indexes, at most 64) compiled to OP_object and an OP_define_field per field: a property
definition with its checks and a shape lookup per field, and a resize of the property array on the way. It now
compiles to the values in order and one new opcode, OP_object_template, which makes the object with its final
shape, taken from a template object in the constant pool, and moves the values in. The object cannot be reached
before it is complete, so building it at the end is not observable. The parser erases the OP_object and
OP_define_field it emitted with OP_nop, as other rewrites there do.

The opcode is added among the regular opcodes (the temporary ones share the numbers after OP_nop), so the opcodes
after it are renumbered: BC_VERSION becomes 29, the built-ins compiled to bytecode (builtin-*.h) are regenerated
with this qjsc (qjsc -C -ss, as `make codegen` does), and the bjson test corpus gets the new version byte.

